        <ArticleTitle>Robotic-assisted pelvic organ prolapse surgery.</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>1200-3</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This study describes technical aspect and short-term results of pelvic organ prolapse surgery using the da Vinci robotic system.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">During a 1-year period, 18 consecutive patients with pelvic organ prolapse were operated on using the da-Vinci system. Clinical data were prospectively collected and analyzed.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All but one procedure was successfully completed robotically (95%). Performed procedures were colpohysteropexy (n = 12), mesh rectopexy (n = 2), or sutured rectopexy combined with sigmoid resection (n = 4). Average setup time was 21 min and significantly decreased with experience. Mean operative time was 172 min (range, 45-280). There were no mortality and no specific morbidity due to the robotic approach. Mean hospital stay was 7 days. At 6 months, all patients were free of pelvic organ prolapse and stated that they were satisfied with anatomical and functional results.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our experience indicates that using the da-Vinci robotic system is feasible, safe, and effective for the treatment of pelvic organ prolapse.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
